.\" The `mc rcon` subcommand. /usr/bin/rcon has its own page, rcon(1).
.\" Named mc-rcon.1 because `mc man rcon` resolves a plugin command to
.\" mc-<plugin name>(1) — see mc-plugins(5).
.TH MC-RCON 1 "" "mc" "mc manual"
.SH NAME
mc-rcon \- RCON console and graceful shutdown for mc
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B mc rcon
.RI [ command ...]
.br
.B mc rcon enable
.br
.B mc rcon disable
.br
.B mc rcon status
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.B mc-rcon
package adds the
.B mc rcon
subcommand to
.BR mc (1),
and the lifecycle hooks that make a stop graceful and a backup consistent. RCON
is Minecraft's remote console protocol, and it is what lets mc talk to a server
that is already running.
.PP
With no arguments,
.B mc rcon
opens an interactive console. With arguments, it runs one command and prints
the reply:
.PP
.RS
.nf
mc rcon list
mc rcon "say server restarting in 5 minutes"
.fi
.RE
.PP
.BR enable ", " disable " and " status
are mc's own verbs and shadow any server command of the same name. None exist
today; if that changes,
.B mc rcon -- <command>
sends one literally.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B mc rcon \fR[\fIcommand\fR...]
Open a console, or run one command. Needs root, or membership of the
.B minecraft
group.
.TP
.B mc rcon enable
Provision a password if there is not one, switch RCON on in
.IR server.properties ,
and report the port. Needs root.
.IP
The server is
.I not
restarted for you. It reads
.I server.properties
at startup, so a restart is required to apply the change \(em but on a
populated server that is a five-minute countdown, and when to spend it is the
operator's call, not a side effect of a config command. Apply it with
.BR "mc restart" .
.TP
.B mc rcon disable
Turn RCON off. The password
.I file
is left alone, so a later
.B enable
restores the same secret rather than inventing a new one; only the copy the
server reads is cleared. Needs root.
.TP
.B mc rcon status
Report whether RCON is configured, which port it resolved, and whether a
connection actually succeeds. Reads only files the
.B minecraft
group can already read, so it needs no root.
.SH "THE PORT"
Resolved in this order:
.IP 1. 3
.B rcon.port
from
.IR /opt/minecraft/server.properties ,
if set.
.IP 2. 3
The game port
.RB ( server-port )
plus 10.
.IP 3. 3
Minecraft's stock port plus 10, that is 25575, when there is no
.I server.properties
yet.
.PP
.B mc rcon status
prints what it resolved.
.SH SECURITY
.B Do not expose the RCON port.
The protocol is unencrypted and unauthenticated beyond a single password, and
every command it accepts runs with full operator rights on the server.
.PP
Two things enforce that here. The client refuses any host that resolves to a
non-loopback address \(em including a name that resolves to both, since which
one a connection lands on is not something the caller controls. And the
password is read from
.I /etc/minecraft/server.passwd
.RB ( "root:minecraft 0640" )
rather than from a command line, because
.I /proc/<pid>/cmdline
is world-readable and would expose it to every local user for the lifetime of
the process.
.PP
Firewall the port anyway.
.SH HOOKS
Installing this package changes three things beyond the console. All of them
are hooks core dispatches \(em see
.BR mc-plugins (5).
.TP
.B pre-stop
Stopping warns players. With at least one player online, or when the count
cannot be determined, the stop is announced 5 minutes, 3 minutes and 1 minute
before it happens. A provably empty server stops immediately.
.IP
The unit's
.B TimeoutStopSec=
is derived from this schedule and asserted by a test. Re-tiering the countdown
without recomputing that value means a SIGKILL through the JVM's chunk flush.
.TP
.BR pre-backup ", " post-backup
Backups are consistent. The world is flushed and held still
.RB ( save-off ", " save-all )
for the duration of the archive, then saving is turned back on \(em whether or
not the archive succeeded.
.TP
.BR post-install ", " post-upgrade
RCON is configured for a newly installed server, with a generated password.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/minecraft/server.passwd
The password.
.BR "root:minecraft 0640" .
.TP
.I /opt/minecraft/server.properties
Holds
.BR enable-rcon ,
.B rcon.port
and
.BR rcon.password ,
which mc owns and re-applies after anything else writes that file.
.TP
.I /usr/libexec/mc/mc-rcon
The plugin executable. Invoked by mc, not by you.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR mc (1),
.BR rcon (1),
.BR mc-plugins (5),
.BR mc-config (5)
